# Nimbli Responsive Design Rules

## Purpose

Nimbli already has a completed and approved desktop interface.

The desktop version is considered the source of truth and must be treated as production-ready.

The purpose of responsive development is to make the application usable on tablets and mobile devices without changing the desktop experience.

Desktop layouts, styling, interactions, and workflows must remain unchanged unless explicitly requested.

The goal is adaptation, not redesign.

---

# Core Principles

## Desktop Is Locked

The existing desktop experience is considered complete.

Never modify desktop layouts, spacing, typography, navigation structure, colors, branding, or interactions unless explicitly requested.

Any responsive implementation should have zero visual impact on desktop screens.

When in doubt:

Preserve desktop behavior.

---

## Add, Don't Replace

Responsive work should add new behavior for smaller screens.

Do not rewrite existing desktop code solely to support responsiveness.

Do not replace working desktop layouts.

Do not remove desktop styles.

Do not redesign existing screens.

Prefer:

- Media queries
- Responsive utility classes
- Flex wrapping
- Grid adaptations
- Conditional styling

Avoid:

- Rebuilding existing layouts
- Rewriting components from scratch
- Large refactors without necessity

---

## Desktop First Strategy

This project follows a desktop-first responsive strategy.

Default styles should represent the desktop experience.

Responsive adjustments should only be applied at smaller breakpoints.

Suggested breakpoints:

- Desktop: 1025px and above
- Tablet: 769px - 1024px
- Mobile: 0px - 768px

---

## Preserve Visual Identity

Never change:

- Colors
- Typography
- Icons
- Branding
- Shadows
- Border radius
- Visual hierarchy
- Design language

Responsive work should only improve usability on smaller screens.

The application should look like the same product across all screen sizes.

---

## Preserve Functionality

Every feature available on desktop must remain available on tablet and mobile.

Never remove functionality because it is difficult to fit on smaller screens.

Instead:

- Stack content vertically
- Collapse sections
- Use drawers
- Use accordions
- Use horizontal scrolling when appropriate
- Use modals when appropriate

Functionality must always be preserved.

---

## React Component Rules

When editing React components:

- Prefer CSS-only responsive solutions.
- Avoid duplicating components for desktop and mobile.
- Avoid creating separate mobile versions unless absolutely necessary.
- Keep component logic unchanged whenever possible.
- Avoid touching business logic during responsive work.

Responsive updates should focus primarily on presentation.

---

## Responsive Adaptation Strategy

When converting desktop layouts:

### Multi-column layouts

Convert to single-column layouts on mobile.

### Horizontal sections

Stack vertically when space becomes limited.

### Buttons

Allow buttons to become full width on mobile when beneficial.

### Forms

Inputs should expand to available width.

### Cards

Cards should stack vertically before shrinking excessively.

### Tables

Prefer horizontal scrolling before removing information.

### Content

Avoid horizontal page scrolling whenever possible.

---

## Before Making Changes

Before implementing any responsive solution, evaluate:

1. Does this affect desktop?
2. Can this be solved with a media query?
3. Can this be solved with flex-wrap?
4. Can this be solved by stacking content?
5. Can this be solved without changing component logic?

Always choose the least invasive solution.

---

## Required Reporting

Whenever responsive changes are proposed or implemented, clearly explain:

- Which breakpoint is affected.
- Which components are affected.
- Whether desktop behavior changes.

Desktop impact should normally be:

"None."

---

# Navigation Requirements

## Desktop Navigation

The desktop navigation must remain unchanged.

Desktop behavior:

- Use the existing left sidebar.
- Sidebar remains permanently visible.
- Sidebar remains fixed on the left side of the application.
- No hamburger menu on desktop.
- No collapsing behavior on desktop.
- No auto-hide behavior on desktop.
- No responsive navigation logic should affect desktop screens.

Desktop navigation is considered locked.

---

## Mobile Navigation

For tablet and mobile devices:

- Replace the desktop sidebar with a top navigation bar.
- Display a hamburger menu icon.
- Navigation items should be accessible through the hamburger menu.
- The mobile navigation must contain all navigation options available on desktop.
- Routes and permissions must remain unchanged.

The navigation structure must remain identical across all devices.

Only the presentation may change.

---

## Mobile Navigation Drawer

Preferred behavior:

- Hamburger button opens a slide-out navigation drawer.
- Drawer may slide from the left side.
- Drawer should close when:
  - A navigation item is selected.
  - The user taps outside the drawer.
  - The user explicitly closes it.

Use smooth animations.

Avoid abrupt transitions.

---

## Smart Navigation Visibility

Mobile devices should maximize available screen space.

Behavior:

### Initial State

- Navbar is visible when the page loads.

### Scrolling Down

- When the user scrolls downward, hide the navbar.
- Hide using a smooth animation.
- Do not leave empty space where the navbar was.

### Scrolling Up

- When the user scrolls upward, even slightly, immediately show the navbar again.
- Reappearance should feel responsive.
- The user should not need to scroll a large distance.

### Top Of Page

- When near the top of the page, the navbar should always remain visible.

---

## Scroll Detection Logic

Preferred implementation:

- Store the previous scroll position.
- Compare current scroll position with previous scroll position.
- If current position is greater than previous position:
  - User is scrolling down.
  - Hide navbar.
- If current position is less than previous position:
  - User is scrolling up.
  - Show navbar.

Use throttling or requestAnimationFrame if necessary to avoid performance issues.

Avoid flickering.

---

## Desktop Protection

Scroll-based navbar behavior must never affect desktop screens.

Desktop behavior:

- Sidebar remains visible.
- Sidebar remains fixed.
- Sidebar never hides.
- Sidebar never collapses automatically.

---

# Nimbli-Specific Requirements

Nimbli is a rehabilitation platform for children and healthcare professionals.

The desktop experience has already been designed and approved.

Treat the desktop application as locked.

Responsive work should focus on:

- Mobile phones
- Tablets
- Smaller laptops

The objective is to improve usability on smaller screens while preserving the existing desktop experience.

Never redesign screens without explicit instructions.

When uncertain:

Preserve desktop behavior and add responsive adaptations only.

---

# Golden Rule

Desktop is the source of truth.

Do not redesign.

Do not improve.

Do not modernize.

Do not refactor unnecessarily.

Only make the existing Nimbli experience work beautifully on smaller screens while keeping desktop visually identical.